WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.890
Not long after I had returned to England, my family and I said our good-byes again.

2
00:00:06.891 --> 00:00:13.601
On June 20, 1702, I left England on a ship called the Adventure.

3
00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:18.246
It was going to sail around Africa to India.

4
00:00:18.247 --> 00:00:23.412
The winds were good on the way to the Cape of Good Hope in southern Africa.

5
00:00:23.413 --> 00:00:27.134
When we arrived in the harbor, the captain said,

6
00:00:27.135 --> 00:00:29.570
"We’ll stay here until March."

7
00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:32.486
"But why?" I asked.

8
00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:36.084
"The ship needs some repairs," he explained.

9
00:00:36.085 --> 00:00:39.283
"And the winds are not good now for sailing to India.

10
00:00:39.284 --> 00:00:41.380
The March winds will be better."

11
00:00:41.381 --> 00:00:44.388
"I’m not pleased," I said.

12
00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:46.736
"I want to see India."

13
00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:50.366
"Can’t you explore this place?" said the captain.

14
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:52.799
"There is much to see here."

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.817
The captain was right.

16
00:00:54.818 --> 00:01:00.248
In Africa I met fascinating people and saw some amazing animals.

17
00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:03.905
But I was impatient to continue our trip.

18
00:01:03.906 --> 00:01:10.018
Finally, in March of 1703, the Adventure set sail for India.

19
00:01:10.019 --> 00:01:17.084
The winds were good until we got near Madagascar, an island off the east coast of Africa.

20
00:01:17.085 --> 00:01:20.109
Then a strong wind began to blow.

21
00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.605
The sea was very rough and tossed our little ship around for twenty days.

22
00:01:25.606 --> 00:01:28.965
Then, suddenly, the wind stopped.

23
00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:32.432
"This perfect calm is very pleasant,"

24
00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:37.349
I said to the captain, who had sailed the oceans for many years.

25
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.713
"It won’t last long," he replied with a worried look.

26
00:01:41.714 --> 00:01:44.894
"I think a bad storm will begin soon."

27
00:01:44.895 --> 00:01:49.750
To the crew he shouted, "Prepare for rough seas tomorrow!"

28
00:01:49.751 --> 00:01:56.339
As the captain had predicted, the next day the wind got stronger and stronger.

29
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.416
The crew adjusted the sails as the violent storm raged.

30
00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:04.804
The fierce wind howled and the waves broke over the ship.

31
00:02:04.805 --> 00:02:11.205
After many hours, the wind finally became less violent and the sea became calmer.

32
00:02:11.206 --> 00:02:16.106
The storm had blown the Adventure hundreds of kilometers to the east. "Where

33
00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:19.855
are we?" I asked the captain. "I

34
00:02:19.856 --> 00:02:26.277
can’t tell for sure," he said, "but our supply of water is now very low. We

35
00:02:26.278 --> 00:02:30.117
must reach land soon and fill our water barrels." Many

36
00:02:30.118 --> 00:02:34.177
days passed and we saw nothing but the sea. At

37
00:02:34.178 --> 00:02:39.350
last, one morning a sailor cried out, "Land!" Everyone

38
00:02:39.351 --> 00:02:42.673
on the ship was happy to hear that word. The

39
00:02:42.674 --> 00:02:45.188
ship sailed closer to the land. We

40
00:02:45.189 --> 00:02:49.845
could not tell whether it was a large island or a continent. "You

41
00:02:49.846 --> 00:02:55.767
men," said the captain to some of the crew, "take the rowboats and go ashore. Fill

42
00:02:55.768 --> 00:02:58.644
the water barrels with fresh water." "Captain,"

43
00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:03.435
I said, "may I go with them to take a look at this place?" "Of

44
00:03:03.436 --> 00:03:05.865
course," he replied. "You

45
00:03:05.866 --> 00:03:08.437
can tell me later what it is like." When

46
00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:12.965
the rowboats reached the shore, one of the sailors said, "I

47
00:03:12.966 --> 00:03:16.157
see no rivers or streams nearby. We

48
00:03:16.158 --> 00:03:20.447
will have to walk along the shore to look for fresh water." "I’m

49
00:03:20.448 --> 00:03:24.895
going to walk inland to explore a little," I told them. "I

50
00:03:24.896 --> 00:03:27.610
will meet you back here in an hour." I

51
00:03:27.611 --> 00:03:31.752
walked about a kilometer, enjoying being on land again. Then

52
00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:35.492
I turned and walked back to meet the crew. When

53
00:03:35.493 --> 00:03:39.587
I arrived on the beach, the sailors were not there. They

54
00:03:39.588 --> 00:03:44.637
were in the rowboats, rowing as fast as they could toward the Adventure .

55
00:03:44.638 --> 00:03:50.796
I was going to shout out to them, when I saw a huge giant walking through the sea.

56
00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:53.691
It was trying to catch up with the rowboats.

57
00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:59.837
The sailors were almost at the ship, and I could see that the giant would not catch them.

58
00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:02.852
I did not wait to see what happened next.

59
00:04:02.853 --> 00:04:08.118
I ran as fast as I could in the other direction.

